  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 AM MDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nThe region will remain under a predominantly southwest flow\npattern aloft and low level south to southeast winds. Both will be\nbringing some moisture into the region. Currently there is a\nweakening upper low that is near San Diego and will be slowly\nlifting NE with some subtle disturbances moving out ahead of it.\nThese little waves will bring on and off showers with a\nthunderstorm also possible to areas mainly west of a KALM-KELP\nline. Nothing more than low scattered coverage. NBM seemed to\nunder do the PoPs early on and wanted at least some mention of\nprecipitation for these areas. Low and high temperatures will be\non the rise with increasing moisture and southerly low level flow.\nHighs will be in the mid 70s to mid 80s for the lowlands with lows\nremaining in the mainly in the 50s, although a few 40s possible\ntonight. \n\nFor Tue-Wed, we will be more in a transitioning period with first\nsystem out of the area but a stronger upper low will be moving\ndown the CA coast. Besides some light showers out west, drier\nconditions return with highs continuing to warm. Upper flow turns\nslightly more S than SW and taps better moisture from south of \nthe Gulf of CA. As we go into Thu this moisture will meet better\nlift over the Borderland ahead of the upper low and we will see\nmore widespread precipitation developing which could continue into\nthe weekend. Temperatures will fall back into the 70s. Do not see\nany mountain snow chances as the southerly flow keeps mid level\ntemperatures very mild.",
